# {{name}} — Agent Context

This file is read by Claude Code (and any other agent) at session start.
It records what is true about this product so that agents do not have to
re-derive it.

## Platform

Built on [Fiducial](https://github.com/AleksaZCodes/fiducial) {{version}}.

- `fid dash` — one read-only view of this product's state; start here
- `fid doctor` — check for drift before starting work
- `fid derive` — run pipelines; `--check` fails CI on stale artifacts
- `fid graph` — the facts → pipelines → artifacts DAG
- `fid upgrade` — pull upstream template and package updates

`fid dash --json` is the machine-readable form of the same facts. Prefer it over
re-deriving product state by reading files: it already reports what is stale,
which pipelines exist, what decisions have been recorded, and whether CI guards
artifact freshness.

## Principles

These are the platform's, not this product's. They are restated here because an
agent working in this repository needs them and should not have to go and find
the platform to get them.

**1 · Declare each fact once; derive every artifact from it.**
A value used in two places belongs in one declared location with the second use
generated. If you are about to type the same value a second time, stop and find
its declaration. A physical fact carries its **unit** and its **tolerance** —
the questions that matter are answered by stacking tolerances, not comparing
nominal values.

**1b · A judgment that cannot be derived is still declared.**
Decisions and their reasoning go in `docs/specs/`, date-stamped and append-only.
Undocumented judgment gets re-litigated — by you in six months, and by every
agent after you.

**1c · A user-visible string is a fact.**
Declared once; every locale is a derivation that must exist. A missing
translation is a missing artifact, not a fallback, and it fails the build.
**Localized by construction** — monolingual is a state this product passed
through before its first commit, not one it ships.

**2 · Push behavior down to the layer with the longest reach.**
Logic in a `no_std` Rust core runs in a browser, on a desktop, at the edge and on
a microcontroller. Logic in a UI framework survives until you change frameworks.
It costs the same to write.

**3 · No abstraction without an escape hatch.**
Every generated artifact is human-readable; every high-level declaration has a
documented override. An abstraction you cannot escape is a trap.

**4 · Everything explicit, in git, legible to a machine.**
State living only in someone's head, a vendor dashboard or a chat log cannot be
derived from, reviewed, reverted or reasoned about. This is what makes
autonomous agent work safe rather than hopeful.

**5 · Cost is a design variable, declared before the design.**
A cost ceiling is a fact set first, with the assertion failing until the design
meets it. Constraints are the mechanism of invention, not a tax on it.

**5b · Spend upfront where it compounds down.**
Effort invested in the system is repaid by every product built on it, so the
correct time to pay is early — driving the marginal cost of the next product
toward zero.

**5c · Order work by cost of delay, not by value.**
Something belongs early when waiting makes it *more expensive*, not when it is
most wanted. Debt-accruing work first (its cost grows while you wait),
multiplying work second (it makes later work cheaper), terminal work last
(it costs the same whenever). Ranking by value alone schedules the compounding
work last, where it costs most.

**6 · Commit to contracts, not to tools.**
One contract per domain, fixed; the tool behind it, swappable. This is what
keeps the system opinionated without being locked in.

**7 · The system compounds.**
A bug fixed once propagates to every product sharing the code. A fix that cannot
propagate is only half-finished.

## Product layout

```
{{name}}/
├── fiducial.toml   — product config, capabilities, guard rules
├── fiducial.lock   — template version tracking (do not edit by hand)
├── MISSION.md      — what this product is for
└── AGENTS.md       — this file
```

## Agents

Two specialised agents are pre-configured in `.claude/agents/`. Both use Opus.

| Slash command | When to use |
|---|---|
| `fiducial-design` | Architecture decisions, brainstorming, new capability design |
| `fiducial-review` | Review the current diff before committing or opening a PR |
| `/fiducial:platform` | Load full platform context (via Fiducial plugin) |

The default session model (Sonnet) handles implementation. Switch to
`fiducial-design` or `fiducial-review` when the task is judgment, not code.

The names are namespaced on purpose: a subagent's filename is its identity, so
an agent called `design` collides with any other `design` agent you have, and
the loser is silently unavailable.

A CI job (`claude-review.yml`) runs an Opus review automatically on every PR.
It requires `ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` in the repository's Actions secrets.

## Guard rules

The `PreToolUse` guard (`fid guard-check`) is contributed by the Fiducial Claude Code
plugin (`.claude/settings.json`). It enforces the rules listed in `fiducial.toml [guard]`.
Rules fire only when a forbidden command is in **command position** — not when it
appears inside a quoted string or as an argument.

## What not to do

- Do not hand-edit files listed in `fiducial.lock` (they are template-tracked).
- Do not commit directly to the default branch (`no-direct-main-push` rule).
- Do not hand-write derived artifacts — change the upstream declaration and
  re-run `fid derive`.
